{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792762888,"nonce":"cPYazYl6KFYrwwUpLWXJdmOtInc=","title":"Interpreter: float bitcasts preserve bits instead of numeric integer conversion","message":"While implementing TypeScript function generic bounds, a constrained scalar is represented as a cast from symbolic T to its scalar bound. After monomorphization this can become an F64-to-F64 Bitcast. The interpreter currently funnels Float through value_to_i64, which numerically converts 2.0 to integer 2, then reinterprets those bits as F64. A generic add(2,4) prints 3e-323 instead of 6; square(3) prints 0 instead of 9.\n\nThis is a compiler correctness defect independent of TypeScript. Identity float bitcasts must preserve the operand, and float-to-integer bitcasts must retain the original IEEE bits, including signed zero and NaN payloads. The constant folder can forward same-type bitcasts to avoid redundant instructions after specialization. Preserve true cross-type bitcasts.\n\nValidate F32/F64 identity and bit round-trips without relying on optimization, then exercise the affected generic arithmetic in interpreter, Cranelift, LLVM and mixed-tier calls. Full official TypeScript corpus regression checking is required before the change is committed.","files":null}]}